1. d4 {Notes by Morphy} d5 2. Bf4 {This move, has , we think,
been unjustly censured, as with the best after-play on both
sides, the second player does not appear to obtain the
slightest advantage. } c5 3. e3 Nc6 4. Nf3 Bg4 5. Be2 Bxf3
6. Bxf3 e6 7. c4 Nf6 8. Nc3 cxd4 9. exd4 dxc4 {Black only
gains the pawn temporarily, as White is enabled presently to
recover it by first capturing Knight with Bishop, and then
moving Qa4. } 10. O-O Be7 {Had Black here moved 10...Nd5, the
following would have been the probable continuation: 11.Bxd5
exd5 12.Nb5 Rc8 13.Re1+ Be7 (best) 14.Nd6+ winning the
exchange. } 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Qa4 O-O 13. Qxc4 Rc8 14. a3 Bd6
{Nd5, at once strikes us as better play. } 15. Bg3 Bxg3
16. fxg3 Nd5 17. Rae1 Qg5 18. Ne4 Qe7 19. b4 a5 20. Nc5 axb4
21. axb4 Rb8 22. Na6 Rb6 23. b5 Qa3 {The correct move,
ensuring the immediate gain of a pawn. 23.cxb5 would have
given Black no advantage. Suppose 23...cxb5 24.Qxd5 exd5 (If
24...Rxa6 25.Qxb5. and the game is even) 25.Rxe7 Rxa6 26.Rd7
and White must now win d-pawn, after which his game will be
fully as good as his antagonist's. } 24. Qc5 Qxc5 25. Nxc5
Rxb5 26. Nd7 Rc8 27. Ne5 Rb7 28. Rc1 Rbc7 29. Rc5 f6 30. Nc4
Kf8 31. Ra1 Ke7 32. Ra6 Nb4 33. Ra4 Rb8 34. Na5 Nd3 35. Kf1
Nxc5 36. dxc5 Rb5 0-1